WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:15.667 --> 00:00:19.033
Hello! I am Mike Bishop from the University of California, San Francisco.

00:00:19.067 --> 00:00:24.500
I am going to tell you the story of how I became a scientist and where that led me.

00:00:24.533 --> 00:00:33.833
It's a rather improbable story, which has a few lessons in it about becoming a scientist and the process of science.

00:00:33.867 --> 00:00:41.000
I grew up, I spent my childhood in a town of 400 in rural Pennsylvania.

00:00:41.033 --> 00:00:45.033
My father was a Lutheran pastor with two very small parishes.

00:00:45.067 --> 00:00:49.900
My family valued academic achievement, but science was not a presence.

00:00:49.933 --> 00:00:57.700
I cannot remember even in the remotest of my extended family someone involved in science,

00:00:57.733 --> 00:01:00.100
and it was never a topic of conversation in the household.

00:01:00.133 --> 00:01:06.099
My first eight years of schooling were in a two room schoolhouse with one teacher

00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:11.033
handling grades 1 through 4 and  another teacher handling grades 5 through 8.

00:01:11.067 --> 00:01:15.566
The second teacher was a remarkable individual who awakened my intellect,

00:01:15.567 --> 00:01:20.566
but again, there was nothing said of science other than the collection of wildflowers.

00:01:20.567 --> 00:01:26.400
My high school was also small, perhaps 80 in the class and maybe 5 of us went to college.

00:01:26.433 --> 00:01:34.500
The culture was dominated by athletics, no surprise there, and pubertal hormones.

00:01:34.533 --> 00:01:40.533
But still, the faculty again recognized my academic interests and encouraged me.

00:01:40.567 --> 00:01:48.700
When I graduated I remember thanking my track coach for the varsity letter that I had finally earned in my senior year,

00:01:48.733 --> 00:01:51.267
and which I actually still have at home.

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:57.667
And he responded by saying that he thought it was more important that I had been valedictorian of the class,

00:01:57.700 --> 00:02:02.133
and he would rather be thanked for the physics course he had taught me,

00:02:02.167 --> 00:02:05.233
which, come to think of it, was not that bad.

00:02:05.267 --> 00:02:13.467
I went off to a small liberal arts college, Gettysburg College, on the famous Civil War battlefield.

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:19.033
And that was a glorious time for me.

00:02:19.067 --> 00:02:28.700
The faculty were devoted and accessible. I dabbled widely in the liberal arts. I took enough science, but not a lot.

00:02:28.733 --> 00:02:33.366
And I gave no thought to being a scientist.

00:02:33.367 --> 00:02:37.399
While I was in high school a family physician took me under his wing

00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:41.367
and began to encourage me towards a career in medicine.

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:48.633
So I took the requisite pre-medical courses but still was not certain about my vocation.

00:02:48.667 --> 00:02:58.299
To dramatize how naive I was, during my junior year, one of my most favored professors

00:02:58.300 --> 00:03:01.500
called me in and asked me what I wanted to do.

00:03:01.533 --> 00:03:05.833
He wanted to counsel me about where to go next.

00:03:05.867 --> 00:03:13.267
And I told him that I was going to go medical school, but that I was interested in an academic career.

00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:18.400
And his response was well then you should consider going to Harvard.

00:03:18.433 --> 00:03:20.833
And my response was, "where is that?"

00:03:20.867 --> 00:03:23.733
I had literally never heard of the place.

00:03:23.767 --> 00:03:30.433
In due course, I went off to Harvard, and Harvard was a revelation.

00:03:30.467 --> 00:03:37.833
First of all, it was in Boston, where I could sate my burgeoning appetite for the fine arts.

00:03:37.867 --> 00:03:42.833
And secondly, for the first time research permeated the education environment.

00:03:42.867 --> 00:03:45.699
It was spoken of repeatedly by our faculty.

00:03:45.700 --> 00:03:51.232
And more importantly, among my peers in my class were individuals

00:03:51.233 --> 00:03:54.367
who had had sophisticated research experience as an undergraduate

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:59.267
and had a profound influence on my aspirations.

00:03:59.300 --> 00:04:03.767
By the end of my second year of medical school, I was at a loss as to what I was going to do.

00:04:03.800 --> 00:04:07.800
It was becoming clear to me that I did not want to practice medicine.

00:04:07.833 --> 00:04:11.700
I had never experienced research. What to do?

00:04:11.733 --> 00:04:16.599
My instructor in pathology rescued me by offering me a fellowship

00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:20.000
in the pathology department at the Massachusetts General Hospital

00:04:20.033 --> 00:04:26.467
where I would both study and do clinical pathology and tinker in his lab. I took it.

00:04:26.500 --> 00:04:32.700
Who would decline a paid year off from medical school

00:04:32.733 --> 00:04:36.167
free to do almost anything I wanted?

00:04:36.200 --> 00:04:38.766
The autonomy of that year was extraordinary.

00:04:38.767 --> 00:04:47.400
I worked hard, but I also had the opportunity to start teaching myself what I might have to know to become a scientist.

00:04:47.433 --> 00:04:57.133
And from that reading, I became enamored of the newly emerging field known as molecular biology.

00:04:57.167 --> 00:05:02.467
When I returned to medical school, I began to look for a way into that field.

00:05:02.500 --> 00:05:06.466
I realized that I was not sophisticated enough to go to the heart of the matter.

00:05:06.467 --> 00:05:12.766
I would have to find some outer niche of the field for beginner like myself,

00:05:12.767 --> 00:05:16.600
and I was assisted in that by an elective course that I took.

00:05:16.633 --> 00:05:25.099
The teacher in that course was a man named Elmer Pfefferkorn who had a tiny laboratory,

00:05:25.100 --> 00:05:32.633
and he took me in to begin my first research experience during my free time as a third year medical student.

00:05:32.667 --> 00:05:36.967
I had decided to work on animal viruses.

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:40.933
I had decided to work on animal viruses because they were simple.

00:05:40.967 --> 00:05:46.999
They provided an accessible way to understand the cell, but they were still understudied.

00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:51.333
In particular, they had not received much attention from molecular biologists.

00:05:51.367 --> 00:05:57.300
I had a wonderful time during that third year, and decided I just wanted to do research in my fourth year.

00:05:57.333 --> 00:06:04.299
So I went to the dean of... the associate dean of students,

00:06:04.300 --> 00:06:09.733
and told him that I would like to substitute research in the laboratory for my fourth year curriculum.

00:06:09.767 --> 00:06:15.432
He took a deep breath, told me that that might be professional suicide,

00:06:15.433 --> 00:06:19.332
but then agreed as long as I would take a two month clerkship

00:06:19.333 --> 00:06:22.467
in what I might want to do my internship and residency.

00:06:22.500 --> 00:06:29.132
I did that. Two months at the Massachusetts General Hospital in internal medicine,

00:06:29.133 --> 00:06:33.667
and I spent the other ten months doing research in a laboratory.

00:06:33.700 --> 00:06:35.899
It wasn't professional suicide.

00:06:35.900 --> 00:06:39.566
I got an internship and a residency at the Massachusetts General Hospital,

00:06:39.567 --> 00:06:41.732
which was a magnificent experience.

00:06:41.733 --> 00:06:47.633
And then I went off to the National Institutes of Health to finally get some serious research training.

00:06:47.667 --> 00:06:54.300
There I began to work on poliovirus with the same rationale as before.

00:06:54.333 --> 00:07:00.032
Here was a simple organism that utilized the mammalian cell for its replication

00:07:00.033 --> 00:07:07.367
and by studying that replication we could learn things about the cell itself, as well as about the virus.

00:07:07.400 --> 00:07:12.266
I published my first papers, and became friends with a scientist from Germany,

00:07:12.267 --> 00:07:15.433
who invited me to come to Hamburg for a year.

00:07:15.467 --> 00:07:22.367
My wife and I went off for our first experience living abroad, and nothing worked in the laboratory.

00:07:22.400 --> 00:07:28.299
But while I was in Germany, I fell in love with Romanesque architecture, German expressionism,

00:07:28.300 --> 00:07:32.667
and I would say in retrospect the year was an immense success.

00:07:32.700 --> 00:07:36.767
While I was there I received some job offers.

00:07:36.800 --> 00:07:41.200
I never did a job search. Those were different times.

00:07:41.233 --> 00:07:45.899
I had two offers from prestigious institutions on the East Coast

00:07:45.900 --> 00:07:51.500
and one from a place called the University of California, San Francisco in San Francisco.

00:07:51.533 --> 00:07:57.699
I literally had never heard of that place, but my mentor at the National Institutes of Health

00:07:57.700 --> 00:08:01.267
had moved there and was trying to recruit me there.

00:08:01.300 --> 00:08:03.333
So I visited.

00:08:03.367 --> 00:08:06.099
I immediately fell in love with the city,

00:08:06.100 --> 00:08:11.133
and I saw a huge opportunity because at the time University of California, San Francisco,

00:08:11.167 --> 00:08:18.433
widely known as UCSF, was not much more than a trade school,

00:08:18.467 --> 00:08:21.833
but it was aspiring to become a first rate research institution.

00:08:21.867 --> 00:08:29.667
So I was offered glorious laboratory space and the freedom to do as I wished.

00:08:29.700 --> 00:08:33.899
When I arrived at UCSF, I met Warren Levinson,

00:08:33.900 --> 00:08:38.367
who had set up a laboratory to study Rous Sarcoma Virus,

00:08:38.400 --> 00:08:42.232
a tumor virus, a virus that caused sarcomas in chickens

00:08:42.233 --> 00:08:46.533
and could be studied in cell culture in vitro as well.

00:08:46.567 --> 00:08:54.733
At the time, the replication of this virus was a complete mystery.

00:08:54.767 --> 00:08:58.700
The mystery was quickly solved by the discovery of reverse transcriptase.

00:08:58.733 --> 00:09:03.232
Two years after I arrived in San Francisco,

00:09:03.233 --> 00:09:07.733
I was joined by Harold Varmus who came as a postdoctoral fellow.

00:09:07.767 --> 00:09:14.267
We formed a research alliance that lasted for 15 years and a friendship that endures to this day.

00:09:14.300 --> 00:09:16.866
Within four years of forming our alliance,

00:09:16.867 --> 00:09:21.900
we had found a way to pry open the black box of the cancer cell.

00:09:21.933 --> 00:09:29.200
We had discovered that the tumor causing gene of Rous Sarcoma Virus was derived from the normal cell.

00:09:29.233 --> 00:09:33.999
In due course it became apparent that there were many such genes in the normal cell

00:09:34.000 --> 00:09:41.400
that could give rise to cancer genes if changed in some way.

00:09:41.433 --> 00:09:46.566
This discovery launched Harold and me on richly rewarding research careers

00:09:46.567 --> 00:09:52.800
that both of us have written about in books that you can find on Amazon.com.

00:09:52.833 --> 00:09:54.900
When I reflect on my career, four things strike me.

00:09:54.933 --> 00:09:59.333
First of all, it is important to chart your own course.

00:09:59.367 --> 00:10:04.067
That will guarantee your passion for your avocation.

00:10:04.100 --> 00:10:08.532
Second, choose to do something important.

00:10:08.533 --> 00:10:18.900
The principal mark of a great scientist is the ability to recognize an important problem before others have seen it.

00:10:18.933 --> 00:10:22.499
Third, try to do something new.

00:10:22.500 --> 00:10:30.200
Try to help start a new line of research. It carries the highest risk, but also the highest reward.

00:10:30.233 --> 00:10:33.999
And fourth, believe in your ideas.

00:10:34.000 --> 00:10:40.767
Believe in your ideas and pursue them until they are either proven or discredited

00:10:40.800 --> 00:10:48.567
because above all else, science is an adventure in ideas. Thank you very much for listening.

